\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{To ground these arguments, the following section will review key scholarship on memes, trace their role in recent U.S. elections, examine how they are weaponized and connect their circulation to broader patterns of affective polarization.}}{1}{section*.6}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted~(W): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ Derived from the Greek \textit {mimema}, the now famous term was first coined by \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\textcite {dawkins1976selfishgene} in his book \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\citetitle {dawkins1976selfishgene}, referring to small cultural units of transmission that, in analogy to genes, pass from person to person through various means of imitation.}}{1}{section*.7}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(W): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ The term \textit {meme}, coined by \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\textcite {dawkins1976selfishgene}, originally described small cultural units that spread by imitation.}}{1}{section*.8}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted~(W): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ This definition was never static, however. The revival of meme in the Web 2.0 era further complicated it by attributing Internet memes two fundamental characteristics: creative production and intertextuality \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {shifman2013memesdigitalculture}. The former refers to the adaptation and transformation of a meme through imitation or parody, either by mimicry or remix \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {shifman2013memesdigitalculture}, while the latter emphasizes how memes blend different cultural references or contexts.}}{1}{section*.9}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(W): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ In the Web 2.0 era, \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\textcite {shifman2013memesdigitalculture} redefined internet memes as characterized by creative production and intertextuality: users constantly adapt and remix templates while weaving together multiple cultural references and contexts.}}{1}{section*.10}%
